here's something interesting last week I  told you about Republican governor from  Ohio Mike DeWine who essentially  abandoned Donald Trump when it comes to  dealing with coronavirus Mike DeWine  took coronavirus seriously from quite  early on one of the one of the earlier  governors to really take action and he  has seen his approval skyrocket thanks  to not following the Trump doctrine and  following the sort of sensible governor  doctrine on coronavirus we've now  learned that Republican Congressman  Peter King from New York who I am no big  fan of is going to be ignoring the  Republican Party and voting for a  democratic coronavirus relief package  that's expected to be voted on later  today if it may already have been voted  on by the time you listen to today's  show now I have mixed feelings about  this in the sense that it is once again  it's a Republican who's personally  affected by something and now is  behaving rationally and doing the right  and pathetic thing I wish it didn't take  that to get them to behave appropriately  and what I mean by that is Peter King is  a New York congressman he represents one  of the hardest hit areas by the virus  it's New York's second congressional  district this is the South Shore of Long  Island so it's not the absolute  epicenter of New York City's five  boroughs but it's as close as you can be  it's it's Juneau the New York City metro  area without actually being there and  that's why he's going to vote in favor  of this package Republicans have been  talking more and more about what if we  maybe limit relief to blue states  they've been hesitant to do more relief  and a second stimulus but Peter King  can't get away with that his district  has been absolutely crushed so there are  some who are framing this as hero Peter  King abandons his party and does the  right thing well it's more like Peter  King abandons his party on this one bill  because it would be political suicide  for him personally not to since he  represents one of the hardest-hit  congressional districts in the country  if it were the Boston area or Seattle  that needed this particular type of bill  and not his district my guess is he  would not be voting for it he would  simply stick it out with Republicans  Peter King is normally no friend of the  left or of the Democratic Party  and this is one it's it's a horrible  realization that we have to have what it  takes for some of these people to do  something to do the right thing is to be  personally affected he needs to be  reelected in November so that's why he  has to do this now as far as the bill  goes there are actually some really  interesting things in it this is called  the heroes Act three trillion in total  it stands for the health and economic  recovery omnibus emergency solutions Act  heroes Act it would pay a company's  payroll costs up to $100,000 per  employee who has been laid off or  furloughed if the business can  demonstrate that their revenue has  dropped at least 20 percent um it would  add eight hundred and fifty billion in  assistance to state and local  governments 200 billion in hazard pay  for frontline health care workers and  other non-essential workers it would do  another round of 1200 dollar checks to  most Americans it would raise the  payment per child from five hundred  dollars also to twelve hundred dollars  but it would be capped total at six  thousand dollars per family it would  extend the Supplemental or I guess  they're calling it enhanced unemployment  from the federal government through  January 31st of next year and one of the  most interesting components that's  buried in there which I don't actually  think will pass if the bill does pass is  that it will eliminate the ten thousand  dollar cap on what's called salt state  and local taxes for 2020 and 2021 this  is super interesting because one of the  ways that the Trump Republican tax  reform of 2017 hurt those in states like  mostly blue tip blue states with higher  state income taxes is they limited the  state and local tax deduction to ten  thousand dollars so if you live in a  very high tax state or just a blue state  with a reasonable amount of tax you are  limited in what amount of tax you can  write off on your federal return to ten  thousand dollars total this goes  directly at blue states and it tends to  have no effect in some of these red  states that have no state income tax so  it would repeal that for 2020 and  21 which is very very interesting  although analysts agree it is very  unlikely that that suspension of the  $10,000 salt cap would actually make it  into a final bill that passes Senate and  House Conference but in the meantime we  will see how the House votes Peter King  apparently will be voting with Democrats  and we will follow it
 

